NO STARCH PRESS TO PUBLISH THIRD EDITION OF BEST-SELLING "STEAL THIS 
COMPUTER BOOK"
Independent Publisher Builds on Success of Internet Underground Primer

San Francisco, CA--The third edition of Wallace Wang's newly updated "Steal 
This Computer Book" (No Starch Press, ISBN 1593270003, 384 pp., $24.95, May 
2003), takes an eye-opening look at the chaotic world of the cyber 
underground. One of a handful of independent computer book publishers, No 
Starch Press has built their "Steal This Computer Book" series into a 
national bestseller with worldwide distribution and translations in seven 
different languages. The success of "Steal This Computer Book" is a 
testament to the public's fascination with hacker culture and No Starch 
Press' abilty to present what could be very technical information in a 
compelling, easy-to-digest format.

"Steal This Computer Book" has sold over 150,000 copies through two 
editions, and in February 2001 landed on the Publishers Weekly Computer 
Bestseller list. Author Wallace Wang attributes the book's success to its 
mass appeal. "There are plenty of computer books about hacking and 
security," he says. "Most of these books focus on the technical details 
that only hackers or security professionals would understand, while "Steal 
This Computer Book" explains many of the same topics in ways that many 
people can relate to. Other books target audiences that are concerned with 
protecting a server or network, but "Steal This Computer Book" focuses on 
helping readers protect their own personal computer."

As with the second edition, the newly updated "Steal This Computer Book 3" 
explores the hows and whys of subversive computing topics, such as virus 
creation and propagation, circumventing online censors, spam, and online 
stalking. In addition, there are new sections on spyware, hacktivism, war 
driving, rootkits, cyberterrorism, and the pros and cons of firewalls and 
intrusion detection. Bill Pollock, publisher of No Starch Press, credits 
"Steal This Computer Book's" conceptual rather than technical coverage of 
these topics for its success. "Many people have only heard of this stuff. 
'Steal This Computer Book 3' allows them to explore these topics from a 
safe distance while at the same time learning how to avoid many of the 
threats outlined in the book. The Internet is a tremendous resource, but 
things can get a bit wacky--especially for the uninformed."

