'Open source' software frees economically deprived entities   

RECORDER REPORT, 6/4/2003
www.brecorder.com

KARACHI (April 06 2003) : 'Open Source' is a global information 
technology phenomenon that brings the benefits of using and 
developing cutting edge software technology to stakeholders in the 
country, experts of the IT sector observed.  They said that at the 
same time 'Open Source Software' frees economically deprived entities 
from the burden of resorting to software piracy for their genuine 
software needs.  

Since the Government of Pakistan is committing itself to the 
reduction of piracy and protection of intellectual property rights 
(IPR), a programme to make 'open source' technology indigenous in the 
country is strongly needed.  In view of this, the direction set by 
the government, 'Linux' is going to be the cornerstone of this 
initiative, as it is world-wide an 'open source' movement.  

A 'Linux Task Force' is, therefore, being formed under the auspices 
of Ministry of Information Technology under Technology Resource 
Mobilisation Unit (TReMU), experts added.  

They said that TReMU has been designed to raise awareness among 
citizens, economists, technocrats, policy makers and other concerned 
professionals on the importance of IT to the country's economy.  

The objective behind TReMU is to create a mechanism of technical 
review and provide support to the implementation process of key 
projects.  

The project will also mobilise the existing technical expertise of 
private sector specialists to assist the government in technical 
review of projects.  

The main purpose of TReMU is to mobilise technical resources by 
forming working groups in different areas to efficiently lay down the 
procedures and set patterns to implement IT projects according to IT 
Policy and action plan.  

Salman Ansari, Advisor to Minister of Information Technology & 
Telecommunications is heading most of the working groups and is 
authorised to notify each working group, on behalf of the Minister.  

The TReMU not only conceptualises critical IT projects but also 
assists in their successful implementation.  

These IT projects are targeted to benefit the country in general and 
the masses in particular.  

They said that TReMU has created different forums, for leading 
private sector specialists and Government officials, in not just IT 
but all fields in which IT can play a major enabling role, to 
undertake joint brainstorming sessions, research, and make 
recommendations to the executing agencies of IT projects in various 
domains.  

Moreover, TReMU is a unique idea in the country to bring IT 
professionals together with sets of skills from the public as well as 
the private sector and constitute valuable think-tank platform for 
cross-fertilisation of ideas and core inputs of policy making by the 
Government of Pakistan.  

The purpose of the project is to mobilise the technical expertise of 
the private and public sectors and create a forum for interaction for 
technical review and recommendations on key IT & T projects.  

The project will mobilise the human resources of the IT Sector, 
combined with expertise of other sectors, from both private and 
public domains.  

This will speed up the process of conceptualisation and improve the 
quality of projects.  

It is aimed to bring the innovative ideas to practical and 
implementable shape.  

The aim of the project is to create a mechanism of technical review 
and provide support to the implementation process of key projects.  

The project will also mobilise the existing technical expertise of 
private sector specialists to assist the government in technical 
review of the projects.  

At the same time, the project will create a forum for leading private 
sector specialists and government officials, in not just IT but all 
fields in which IT can play a major enabling role, to undertake joint 
brainstorming sessions, research, and make recommendations to the 
executing agencies of IT projects in various domains.  

It will also develop a proper mechanism for technical monitoring of 
implementation of the approved projects.  

The project will have a multidimensional impact. Each working group 
will come up with multiple proposals which can be converted into PC-I 
by concerned government departments with the assistance of experts 
present in the working groups.  

The working group will undertake a detailed analysis of the focus 
sector and undertake pre-proposal feasibility studies from a 
multitude of professional aspects that will ensure greater viability 
of projects.  

The working group in each area will then follow the technical 
progress of the projects in that area, and make recommendations for 
effective implementation.  

This will also enhance IT & T Division's ability to evaluate new 
projects and monitor the progress of existing projects.  

Through the proposed working groups the projects will achieve a forum 
where the private and public sectors can interact and make meaningful 
recommendations on projects.  

The forum will also enable experts from different fields to interact 
where IT has caused a convergence in domains.  

The broad representation will ensure that the projects conceived at 
all levels are thoroughly evaluated from all angles eg a working 
group on establishing call centres will evaluate the projects from 
marketing, training, supply, demand and operations aspects of setting 
up call centres in the country through a single forum.  

The TReMU is a project sponsored by Ministry of Information 
Technology (MoIT) and executed by Comsats Institute of Information 
Technology (CIIT).  

They present proposals and prepare PC-I in collaboration with the 
individuals who are experts in fields in which the project will be 
initiated.  

The PC-l prepared in this regard will cover two types of projects to 
nominate individuals and establish working groups to work towards 
completing the groundwork for the project as well as investigating 
the project.  

The preparation of sub-PC-I for projects will come under the pre-
planned projects, monitor the projects and keep track of the 
deliverables, which will be established at the onset of the project.  


TReMU will also be responsible for ensuring that the projects are 
completed on time.  

The process of making Linux and open source technologies indigenous 
has to follow the open source model itself and hence the 'Linux Task 
Force' is being established consisting of volunteers with the desire 
and capability to participate in the process.  

Professionals in the Task Force will also come out with R&D 
programmes for creating projects to be funded by the government for 
the creation of user-friendly client server software, training 
strategies, usage in the development of applications including local 
language software development, proposal for induction into the 
syllabus, etc as well as focused training programmes.  

Various sub-groups will be formed under the umbrella of the Linux 
task force and they will contribute by initiating various inventive 
projects in different critical areas with regard to Linux.  

Task Force will be responsible for technical review of the proposals 
and PC-Is and will also co-ordinate all technical and administrative 
matters related with Linux with the government.  

The initiative has roots in several discussions that have been taking 
place on the issue of protection of IPR (intellectual property 
rights), adoption of open source as a national strategy, providing a 
basis for creating a skill set to enable people to do original work 
to create a pool of Linux expertise and get into the real nuts and 
bolts issues like embedded software using the Linux platform where 
this seminar is being arranged for the formal launch of the 
initiative.  

The seminar was also attended by Shahid Ansari, Project Manager of 
TreMU, Salman Ansari, Advisor to IT &T Minister, Humayun Bashir, 
Country Manager IBM and others.  


